meta {
  name: Preview a catalog entry
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}{{apiPrefix}}/game-catalog/preview?name=Hollow Knight&igdbId=2932&releaseDate=2017-02-24
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  name: Hollow Knight
  igdbId: 2932
  releaseDate: 2017-02-24
  ~genres: Metroidvania
}

docs {
  What the shared catalog row for this game holds, or would hold if it were added
  right now: `genres` and `releaseDate`.

  A GET rather than part of the add response, because the add form shows these
  before anything is created. It exists because those values belong to the catalog,
  not the adder, so the form can display them but not set them.

  A sub-resource of `game-catalog` rather than a `/me` route. It was
  `GET /me/catalog-preview` until 2026-08-18, which put it under `/me` for an
  authorization reason: it needs auth and burns rate-limit budget. But it describes
  a catalog fact shared by everyone who owns the game, not a fact about the caller,
  so it belongs next to the search instead.

  The SERVICE function stays in `services/me.py` even though the route moved. It
  shares its genre-resolution helpers with the add path deliberately, so the
  preview and the write cannot disagree about the rule.

  Pass the `name`, `genres` and `releaseDate` the client already has from IGDB.
  For a new IGDB row the add re-fetches all three from IGDB rather than trusting
  them, and this preview does not, so it matches the add only when they are passed
  through from a search result unedited, as the form does.

  Watch the query parameter names. CamelModel's camelCase convention covers SCHEMA
  fields, not bare query params, so `igdbId` and `releaseDate` only work because
  the route declares explicit aliases. Without them FastAPI silently ignores the
  camelCase spelling and the preview runs as though the game had no IGDB id. That
  shipped once.

  Another GET that writes, in this case rate-limit counters, since it can call out
  to Wikipedia. Blocked on preview deploys for that reason.

  - 200 `{"genres": [...], "releaseDate": "..."}`
  - 401 not logged in
  - 429 over the per-minute lookup budget
  - 503 writes disabled on this environment
}
